# Customizing the {{ project_upper }} solver

You change the **physics** by editing the config and regenerating. Everything
else is a TOML knob or a hook listed below. Don't hand-edit generated framework
code — it's overwritten on the next regenerate.

## Edit the config, then regenerate
File: `{{ project_name }}.py`

- **Equations** (RHS, constraints) and **analytic initial data** live here.
- **Add a parameter** — one line:
  `eta = dendrosym.dtypes.ParameterVariable("eta", dtype="double")`
  (add `num_params=N, default=[...]` for an array). Regenerating wires it into
  the C++ global, the TOML reader, and the sample TOML for you. Set its value at
  runtime in the TOML `[physics]` section.

## Tune a run — no regenerate
Edit `{{ project_name }}_parameters.sample.toml` (copy it per run): resolution,
domain, CFL, IO/checkpoint frequency, refinement mode, RK type, BH params.

## SIMD cascade RHS kernel (opt-in, no physics change)
The evolution RHS can also be emitted as a *polynomial-cascade* kernel: the
config's own named tensors (inverse metric, Christoffels, Ricci, ...) become
ordered layers, each CSE'd separately, emitted as one width-agnostic VEC body that
`rhs.cpp` walks 4 (AVX2) or 8 (AVX-512) points at a time -- **the width is chosen
when you compile**, from the ISA that `CPU_ARCH` enables, with the flat kernel as
the fallback on a machine without AVX2+FMA. Same equations, same derivatives, same
KO/BCs; only the kernel structure differs (per-layer CSE + SIMD; agreement with
the flat kernel is ~1e-16 relative).

- **Register** (once, in `{{ project_name }}.py`): a layer spec + defaults
  `dendroConfigs.set_cascade_spec_function("evolution", build_specs,
  options=CascadeOptions(enabled=False, simd="avx2"))`.
- **Emit**: `python {{ project_name }}.py --cascade` (or flip `enabled=True`).
  Override any knob from the command line: `--cascade-L 7`,
  `--cascade-no-fma-tree`, `--cascade-inline-threshold 0`,
  `--cascade-global-cse` ... (`python {{ project_name }}.py --help`).
  `--no-cascade` returns to the flat kernel. The flat `*_rhs_eqns.cpp.inc` is
  always generated too, so switching back never regenerates.
- **Check**: `python -m dendrosym.cascade.dendro_bridge --harness
  solver/gencode/{{ project_name }}_evolution_cascade_manifest.json --run`
  compiles a flat-vs-cascade comparison on random data (tolerance 1e-12).
- **Cache caveat**: the gencode cache keys on the equations + cascade options,
  not on the spec function's body. After editing `build_specs` itself, run once
  with `DENDRO_NO_GENCODE_CACHE=1`.
- **Build**: `CPU_ARCH` (`native`, `emeraldrapids`, `znver4`, `haswell`, ...) sets
  `-march`; the generated `*_cascade_select.cpp.inc` then picks AVX-512 if the
  target has `__AVX512F__`, else AVX2 (`__AVX2__` + `__FMA__`), else the flat
  kernel. `cmake -DCASCADE_KERNEL=flat|avx2|avx512` forces one for A/B runs
  (a width the target lacks fails at compile time, not at run time).

## Hooks you can hand-edit
| Want to… | Go to |
|---|---|
| Add a non-analytic (e.g. random) initial-data type | `solver/src/grUtils.cpp` → `initDataFuncToPhysCoords()` switch |
| Add custom output / change the cadence | `solver/{{ project_name }}_main.cpp` → the `while` loop |
| Run per-step / per-stage logic | `solver/src/{{ project_upper }}Ctx.cpp` → `pre_stage` / `post_stage` / `pre_timestep` / `post_timestep` (empty stubs) |
| Change the refinement criterion | `{{ project_upper }}Ctx::is_remesh()` (or just set `{{ project_upper }}_REFINEMENT_MODE` in TOML) |
| Keep raw initial data (skip constraint cleanup) | remove the `enforce_{{ project_name }}_constraints()` call in `{{ project_upper }}Ctx::init_grid()` |

If you're editing generated code not listed here, it probably belongs in the
config or the framework (dendrolib) — ask first.
